ing blank map spaces meant empty land repeats the terra nullius idea that ignored First Nations occupation.. Correction: No. It means the chart-makers had not mapped it yet. First Nations Peoples already lived across the continent.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y4-w03-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise map.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use map with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify coastline and explain why it matters.","I can practise chart independently on a new prompt.","I can notice landmark needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Mapping the Australian coastline was gradual.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the source or place","Mapping the Australian coastline was gradual","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about map with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: students compare a simplified historic coastal chart (school pack) with a modern outline map. They mark that early charts were incomplete and that blank spaces did not mean empty land.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA historic chart leaves a stretch of coast blank. Does that mean no one lived there? Explain.\n\nFEEDBACK\nNo. It means the chart-makers had not mapped it yet. First Nations Peoples already lived across the continent.","explain":"Mapping the Australian coastline was gradual. Charts used landmarks, latitude ideas of the time and names given by visitors. Modern maps still show coasts, but place names and boundaries have histories, including First Nations names that should be confirmed locally.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessi